oindent where $H$ is the Hadamard gate, and $N=2^n$. Assume that the system $\left| {\Psi _0 } ightangle$ is re-written as follows,  egin{equation}  egin{array}{l} \left|\Psi_0ightangle =  rac{1}{{\sqrt N }} \sum\limits_{\scriptstyle j = 0, \hfill \atop \scriptstyle j \in X_T \hfill}^{N - 1} {\left| j ightangle} +  rac{1}{{\sqrt N }} \sum\limits_{\scriptstyle j = 0, \hfill \atop \scriptstyle j \in X_F \hfill}^{N - 1} {\left| j ightangle},\ \end{array} \end{equation} 
oindent where $X_T$ is the set of all balanced assignments of $n$ bits and $X_F$ is the set of all unbalanced assignments. Let $M=\left( { egin{array}{*{20}c} n \ {{	extstyle{n \over 2}}} \ \end{array}} ight)$ be the number of balanced assignments among the $2^n$ possible assignments, $\sin (	heta ) = \sqrt {{M \mathord{\left/ {\vphantom {M N}} ight.\kern-
ulldelimiterspace} N}}$ and $0 < 	heta \le \pi /2$, then the system can be re-written as follows,  egin{equation} \left|\Psi_0ightangle = \sin (	heta )\left| {\psi _1 } ightangle + \cos (	heta )\left| {\psi _0 } ightangle, \end{equation} 
oindent where $\left| {\psi _1 } ightangle= \left| {	au } ightangle$ represents the balanced assignments subspace and $\left| {\psi _0 } ightangle$ represents the unbalanced assignments subspace. Let $D=WR_0 \left( \phi ight)W^\dag R_	au \left( \phi ight)$, $R_0 \left( \phi ight) = I - (1 - e^{i\phi } )\left| 0 ightangle \left\langle 0 ight|$, $R_	au \left( \phi ight) = I - (1 - e^{i\phi } )\left| 	au ightangle \left\langle 	au ight|$, where $W=H^{\otimes n}$ is the Walsh-Hadamard transform \cite{hoyer00}. Iterate the operator $D$ on $\left|\Psi_0ightangle$ for $q$ times to get,  egin{equation} \left| {\Psi _1 } ightangle = D^{q}\left| {\Psi_0 } ightangle = a_q \left| {\psi _1 } ightangle + b_q \left| {\psi _0 } ightangle , \end{equation} 
oindent such that,  egin{equation} a_q = \sin (	heta )\left( {e^{iq\phi } U_q \left( y ight) + e^{i(q - 1)\phi } U_{q - 1} \left( y ight)} ight), \end{equation}  egin{equation} b_q = \cos (	heta )e^{i(q - 1)\phi } \left( {U_q \left( y ight) + U_{q - 1} \left( y ight)} ight), \end{equation} 
oindent where $y=cos(\delta)$, $\cos \left( \delta ight) = 2\sin ^2 (	heta )\sin ^2 ({	extstyle{\phi \over 2}}) - 1$, $0<	heta\le \pi/2$, and $U_q$ is the Chebyshev polynomial of the second kind \cite{ChebPoly} defined as follows,  egin{equation} U_q \left( y ight) =  rac{{\sin \left( {\left( {q + 1} ight)\delta } ight)}}{{\sin \left( \delta ight)}}. \end{equation}

To prepare the entanglement, let  egin{equation}  egin{array}{l} \left| {\Psi _2 } ightangle = \left| {\Psi _1 } ightangle \otimes \left| {0 } ightangle\ \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,= a_q \left| {\psi _1 } ightangle \otimes \left| {0 } ightangle + b_q \left| {\psi _0 } ightangle \otimes \left| {0 } ightangle,\ \end{array} \end{equation} 
oindent and apply a quantum Boolean operator $U_f$ on $\left| {\Psi _2 } ightangle$, where $U_f$ is defined as follows,  egin{equation} U_f \left| {x,0} ightangle = \left\{ { egin{array}{*{20}c} {\left| {x ,0} ightangle ,{m if }\, \left|xightangle \in \left|\psi _0ightangle,} \ {\left| {x ,1} ightangle ,{m if }\, \left|xightangle \in \left|\psi _1ightangle,} \ \end{array}} ight. \end{equation} 
oindent and $f:\left\{ {0,1} ight\}^n 	o \{ 0,1\}$ is an $n$ inputs single output Boolean function that evaluates to True for any $x \in X_T$ and evaluates to False for any $x \in X_F$, then,  egin{equation}  egin{array}{l} \left| {\Psi _3 } ightangle = U_f \left| {\Psi _2 } ightangle \ \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,= a_q \left| {\psi _1 } ightangle \otimes \left| {1 } ightangle + b_q \left| {\psi _0 } ightangle \otimes \left| {0 } ightangle.\ \end{array} \end{equation} Apply measurement $M_1$ on the auxiliary qubit $\left| {ax_1 } ightangle$ as shown in Figure ef{alg}. The probability of finding $\left| {ax_1 } ightangle=\left| {1} ightangle$ is,  egin{equation} Pr{(M_1 = 1)} = \left| {a_q } ight|^2 \ge 0.9975, \end{equation} 
oindent and the system will collapse to,  egin{equation} \left|\Psi_3^{(M_1 = 1)}ightangle = \left| {\psi _1 } ightangle \otimes \left| {1 } ightangle. \end{equation}

